---
term: Charter
section: building-blocks
order: 20
definition: The founding document that creates a corporation over a real commons and defines how its members deliberate, modify the charter, and carry out decisions.
seeAlso:
  - chartered-corporation
  - charter-formation
  - rules-as-code
---
The charter is the fundamental institutional primitive of the Social Charter. Every institution, from a single person to the highest human authority, is an instance of the same chartered form. A charter is an operational specification, not a ceremonial document.

A charter has two fundamental ingredients: a [[commons]] to decide over, such as shared resources or property, and mechanisms for deliberation, self-modification, and execution. Where a real commons exists, members have to make clear decisions about it. Where none exists, there's no need for a charter. A charter can carry symbolism of any kind, but symbolism adds to these ingredients and never replaces them.

At a minimum, a charter defines the following:

- Purpose and [[common interest]]
- Membership
- [[Jurisdiction]]
- [[Asset|Assets]] and [[commons]]
- [[Objective function]]
- Rights and obligations
- [[Delegation]] powers
- Executive authority
- [[Arbitration]] and conflict-resolution mechanisms
- [[Measurement mechanism|Measurement]] and [[audit]] mechanisms
- [[Amendment]] and evolution procedures
- [[Dissolution]] and [[exit]] procedures
- Relationships with parent, constituent, and sibling corporations
